  "narrative": "The dataset now supports five major same-dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss is associated with stronger dependency_SMARCA2, MSI-high with stronger dependency_WRN, KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency_NFE2L2, TP53 loss with weaker dependency_USP7 after independent validation, and CDKN2A loss with stronger dependency_MDM2. The main effect of each accepted association has already been checked extensively, so the next scientifically useful work is specificity testing, co-deletion/context testing, and broadening coverage to less tested predictor-outcome pairs.\n\nFor the newly accepted CDKN2A-loss association with stronger dependency_MDM2, MTAP loss is an important co-deletion probe because MTAP is frequently co-deleted with CDKN2A. A strong MTAP-loss association would suggest that the MDM2 signal may reflect broader 9p21 deletion or linked genomic structure, whereas a near-null MTAP result would increase confidence that the association is more specific to CDKN2A loss or its regulatory products. I also registered missing marker-null direct controls for the newly accepted MDM2 and USP7 effects, for SMARCA2, and as part of the marker-specificity program. Markers D, E, and F have no assigned biology; near-zero associations would argue against the large accepted effects being driven by latent marker-defined structure, whereas substantial marker associations would caution against over-specific biological interpretation.\n\nFor SWI/SNF biology, I registered a direct ARID1A-loss versus dependency_SMARCA2 contrast. The accepted SMARCA4-loss effect is very large, but a direct ARID1A-loss effect would test whether the dependency is a broader SWI/SNF complex feature or more specific to SMARCA4 loss. I also registered SMARCA4 loss versus dependency_POLR2A to test a transcriptional dependency hypothesis: chromatin-remodeler loss may alter RNA polymerase II dependency. RB1 loss versus dependency_DHX9 tests whether replication/transcription stress associated with RB1 loss increases dependence on an RNA helicase resolving R loops. NF1 loss versus dependency_BRAF tests RAS/MAPK bypass: if NF1 loss activates upstream signaling, BRAF could become less essential, though the direction remains biologically contestable. STK11 loss versus dependency_NFE2L2, restricted to KEAP1 wild-type lines, tests whether STK11 loss has an independent association with NRF2-pathway dependency rather than merely reflecting co-occurring KEAP1 loss. Finally, ERBB2 amplification versus dependency_EGFR and a high-Cas9 refinement of RB1 loss versus dependency_KIF18A broaden coverage of RTK coactivation and a borderline mitotic dependency signal. All of these remain associations within the supplied lung-lineage CRISPR dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted main same-dataset associations: H6 SMARCA4_loss -> stronger SMARCA2; H8 MSI_high -> stronger WRN; H17 KEAP1_loss -> weaker NFE2L2; H49 TP53_loss -> weaker USP7, independently validated and closed; H59 CDKN2A_loss -> stronger MDM2, accepted and active for robustness/specificity. Additional accepted/robust technical/context subsets include Avana/non-Avana where feasible, high screen_roc_auc, high Cas9, and KEAP1 effect within STK11_loss. Current priority: (1) specificity of MDM2 effect: MTAP co-deletion and marker-null controls; (2) specificity of USP7 effect: marker F plus existing marker controls; (3) completeness of marker-null controls for accepted outcomes; (4) broad predictor-outcome coverage: ARID1A direct on SMARCA2, SMARCA4 on POLR2A, RB1 on DHX9, NF1 on BRAF, STK11 independent of KEAP1 on NFE2L2, ERBB2 amp on EGFR, and high-Cas9 refinement of RB1 on KIF18A. Existing active queue remains large: H39, H40, H41, H50, H55, H60, H61-H63, H64-H77. Approximate absolute 10% thresholds: ~0.025-0.030 for outcomes near mean -0.25, ~0.041 for SMARCA2; effects near 0.8 are unambiguous on near-zero-scale NFE2L2/USP7. Interpret all conclusions as associations in this dataset, not causal or externally generalizable."
